    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Tuesday, December 31, 2013 12:46:04 PM UTC-5, Terrence Jorgensen wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Monday, December 30, 2013 10:25:26 AM UTC-5, Eric Green wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><div>fit &lt;- cfa(f, data=input, ordered=names(input))</div></div><div><br></div><div>I get an error that &quot;system is computationally singular&quot;. When I run qr(input)$rank, I get a value of 26, which I believe means I have the expected number of linearly independent column vectors. </div><div><br></div><div>If qr(input)$rank == dim(input)[2], why am I getting the &quot;system is computationally singular&quot; error? Or am I misunderstanding something?</div><div><br></div><div><div><br></div></div></div></blockquote><div>qr() assumes you have numeric (basically continuous, interval) data, not categorical.  You are treating the data (appropriately) as categorical when you run lavaan.  The fact that your your rank == 26 means none of the 2-way contingency tables should show evidence redundancy among any pairs of variables.  But I&#39;m not sure where in the &quot;system&quot; the problem occurs.  The more complete error message might give a better clue for Yves to spot something, seeing as it is apparently some object (matrix?) named &quot;B&quot; that can&#39;t be inverted:<br><br><span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\">Error in solve.default(B) : <br>  system is computationally singular: reciprocal condition number = 1.12493e-16<br></span><br><br> Terry<br><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
