     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><blockquote type=\"cite\"><span><div><div>On 12/31/2013 06:46 PM, Terrence Jorgensen wrote:\r\n<br>&gt; Error in solve.default(B) :\r\n<br>&gt;    system is computationally singular: reciprocal condition number =\r\n<br>&gt; 1.12493e-16\r\n<br>\r\n<br>I have found the problem, but I have not found (yet) a robust solution.  \r\n<br>The problem is related to bivariate tables that contain lots of zeroes, eg\r\n<br>\r\n<br> &gt; table(input$y.cdi5.t0,  input$y.cdi8.t0)\r\n<br>\r\n<br>       0   1   2\r\n<br>   0 212  12   9\r\n<br>   1   2   0   0\r\n<br>   2   1   0   0\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Usually, this is not problematic, but in this case, it clearly is.  \r\n<br>Perhaps because there are several sparse bivariate tables, all involving  \r\n<br>the same variable(s). Removing one of these variables (eg y.cdi5.t0)  \r\n<br>seems to solve the problem.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Yves.\r\n<br>\r\n<br></div></div></span></blockquote></div>"
